  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1230 AM MDT Wed Aug 19 2026\n\nWe still have a few showers out there early this morning, but these \nshould be gone by sunrise. It finally looks like we will get a break \nfrom the monsoon, but it is like a shark. It is only leaving for a \nbrief interlude, it will be back for more, but there will be\nbreak.\n\nThe main concern the next few days looks like it will \nshift to more hot temperatures. A ridge across the desert \nsouthwest will slowly build to the north, and bring a gradual \nrise in temperatures over the next few days. The rise in \ntemperatures begins today, with widespread mid to upper 80s \nacross most of the lower elevations, with a few locations \npossibly touching 90. As for precipitation, we can't completely\nrule it out but the chance will be close to zero for 95 percent\nof the area. We did add some slight chance POPs across southern\nLincoln and western Sweetwater Counties where just enough \nmoisture may linger for an isolated shower. With the dry low \nlevels, it would likely only be virga. But even here, the \nchance is only 1 out of 6. Normal to below normal precipitable \nwater levels should keep the rest of the area rain free. There \nis one other concern. There will be a bit of breeze developing \nin southern zones, and relative humidity will be falling to \naround 15 percent. This raises some concern for fire weather. At\nthis time, the chance of getting 3 hours of critical fire \nweather is less than 1 out of 3, so we will not issue any fire \nweather highlights. Elevated fire weather will be likely for a \nfew hours this afternoon though.\n\nAs we head to Thursday and Friday, the main concern will be heat as \n700 millibar temperatures approach 20 celsius in portions of the \narea. On Thursday. most of the population centers below 6000 feet \nwill have at least a 1 in 2 chance of high temperatures over 90. \nFriday still looks like the hottest day, with locations below 6500 \nfeet having at least a 3 out of 5 chance of high temperatures over \n90. Areas below 5000 feet will have a 1 in 2 chance of high \ntemperatures over 95. The good news is this will be Wyoming heat, \nmeaning humidity will remain low, meaning it should not be dangerous \nheat. As for potential convection, chances will increase each day as \nmonsoonal moisture begins to creep back in. However, as per the \nusual progression, it will be largely at the mid levels first. \nThrough Friday, most convection would be confined to the mountains \nwith nothing more than a 1 out of 3 chance in any particular\nspot. And with many of the showers, rain may not even reach the\nground. As for fire weather concerns, wind should remain light \nto moderate so critical fire weather is not expected despite the\nlow humidity.\n\nWe will should be back in the monsoonal pattern on Saturday as \ndeeper moisture spreads back over the area. Precipitable waters have \nthe potential to climb over 150 percent of normal starting on \nSaturday and continuing through much of next week. All areas will \nhave a chance of showers and storms starting on Saturday. As for the \nmost impacted areas, the majority of guidance favors the maximum \nimpact in western Wyoming this weekend, but confidence on this is \nlow. There will be the usual ebbs and flows in the coverage of \nconvection into next week, and it is difficult to nail down details \nthis far out as to what day will be the most active. As for \ntemperatures, with more cloud cover temperatures should begin to \ncool starting Saturday. Overall though, temperatures should remain \nlargely above average into next week. So after around a three day \nbreak, the monsoonal pattern of diurnal convection likely returns \nthis weekend.",
